    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-70127-6",
    "abstract": "Despite growing evidence that climate extreme events can significantly affect local economies, the implications of cross-regional and planetary-scale dependencies in climate extremes remain inadequately understood. We demonstrate a crucial link between the projected increase in spatially compounding hot, wet, and dry extremes and the amplification of global economic exposure. Based on Earth System Model projections from the 6th phase of the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project, we analyze how planetary-scale and cross-regional dependencies can exacerbate regional disparities in economic exposure. Our findings reveal that regions with lower present-day economic wealth are more likely to face extreme events simultaneously with other areas, amplifying the potential threats to their economic stability. This study highlights the necessity of considering economic exposure to climate extremes beyond local scales, emphasizing the need for assessing cross-regional exposures and understanding the connections between localized exposures and global economic dynamics.",
    "field": "Climate change economics",
    "problem": "The economic implications of spatially compounding climate extremes\u2014the simultaneous occurrence of events in different regions\u2014remain inadequately understood, obscuring how cross-regional dependencies amplify global economic exposure and exacerbate regional disparities.",
    "method": "This study proposes a framework for quantifying economic exposure to spatially compounding climate extremes by intersecting climate model projections of extreme events with gridded gross domestic product (GDP) projections to assess impacts at global, regional, and cross-regional scales.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify how spatially compounding climate extremes (heatwaves, heavy precipitation, droughts, and water scarcity) amplify global economic exposure by linking Earth System Model projections with future socioeconomic scenarios.",
